 At Ajanta , the Buddhist caves were built for a monastery ( vihara ) and Chaitya halls ( prayer halls ) , and their dates range from about 200 BCE to 650 CE . The earliest caves are from the time of the Satavahanas and the latest caves from the Vakataka era . Only three of the caves have inscriptions to date them : Cave 20 , dated to 601 – 02 , contains an edict of Harshavardhana ; Cave 16 is dated to 480 CE ; and Cave 1 has a stupa wall dating back to the early 2nd century CE . Ajanta contains paintings that date from the 2nd century BCE to about 650 CE . Although the painting styles in Ajanta differ greatly , they are considered to be a unique school . The style is characterized by an emphasis on color and composition and a preference for scenes from the Jataka tales . The Ajanta Caves are recognized by UNESCO as a World Heritage
